.TH SWAYDESK-HELP 1 2026-11-15 "swaydesk 0.1.0" "Sway desktop components"
.SH NAME
swaydesk\-help \- the Swaydesk help window: keyboard shortcuts and desktop apps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swaydesk\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
A window with two tabs. Super+F1 and Super+/ open it; the same keys close it again. Typing searches both
tabs, ignoring case and accents. Escape clears the search, and with the search empty it closes the window.
Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Page Up and Ctrl+Page Down switch between the tabs.
Its menu entry is org.swaydesktop.Help.
.PP
.B Shortcuts
lists the key bindings (bindsym, bindcode and bindgesture) of the Sway configuration in use, read when the
window opens. The main file comes from the running Sway (the IPC GET_CONFIG request), so it is the one Sway
loaded: the package's file in the package session, or
.I ~/.config/sway/config
otherwise. The include lines are followed on disk as Sway does (~, environment variables, globs and paths
relative to the including file), so a binding added in
.I ~/.config/sway/config.d/50\-local.conf
shows up by itself. A later binding with the same keys replaces the earlier one, as in Sway. Up to 8 levels
of include, 256 files and 1 MiB per file are read.
.PP
A comment right above a binding describes it, and a section comment groups the bindings below it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
### Windows
## Close the window
bindsym $mod+q kill
.fi
.RE
.PP
A description also covers the bindings right after it, up to a blank line or another line that is not a
comment: Super+1 to Super+9 under one description show as a single row. The descriptions of the shipped
configuration are translated; your own are shown as written. A binding without a description shows its
command. Keys show as Super, Ctrl, Alt and Shift; bindings that also work on the lock screen (\-\-locked)
carry a lock icon.
.PP
.B Using Swaydesk
has a card per part of the desktop (theme, screenshot, screen recording, clipboard, app grid, dock, window
switcher, notifications, lock screen, night light and, when installed, displays), with its current shortcut,
found by the command it runs, and a button that opens it. The button runs a fixed program, never a shell.
A component turned off in the [enabled] table of ~/.config/sway\-desktop/config.toml shows "Off" and how to
turn it back on, without the button.
.PP
The session runs it under the name sway\-help (a link in the package's helper directory to
/usr/bin/swaydesk\-help).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Exit 0 at once without doing anything: the Python launchers use it to check that the binary and its libraries load.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SWAYSOCK
The Sway IPC socket. Without it, or if Sway does not answer, the configuration file is read from disk.
.TP
.B SWAYDESK_SESSION
In the package session (package), the file read from disk is the package's configuration.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sway/config
The Sway configuration of the dotfiles session.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sway/config.d/
Your own additions; their bindings appear in the window.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR swaydesk (7),
.BR sway (5)
